Why Your Rate Matters More Than You Think
A mortgage is a long-term commitment, often spanning 15 to 30 years. Even a small difference in interest rate can translate into tens of thousands of dollars over the life of the loan. Yet many buyers still treat the quoted rate as fixed, accepting the first number they're given without understanding what's actually shaping it.
In reality, several factors influence your mortgage rate — and buyers have more control over them than they often realize:
    • Credit score — a stronger score typically unlocks a lower rate 
    • Loan type — conventional, FHA, VA, and USDA loans each come with different rate structures 
    • Down payment size — a larger down payment can lower both your rate and monthly payment 
    • Debt-to-income ratio — lenders assess how much of your income is already committed to other debt 
    • Loan term — shorter terms, like 15-year loans, generally carry lower rates than 30-year terms 
Huntsville families who take the time to understand these factors before applying often end up in a much stronger negotiating position than those who accept the first offer they receive.
